    # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    # γ (GAMMA) - RECIPROCATION DECAY RATE
    # ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    # Definition: γ = 0.00005 day⁻¹ = 5×10⁻⁵ day⁻¹
    #
    # CRITICAL: Units are day⁻¹ (per day), NOT s⁻¹ (per second)
    # This is a slow decay rate for cosmic-scale magnetic field evolution.
    #
    # Characteristic Decay Time:
    #   τ = 1/γ = 20,000 days ≈ 54.8 years
    #   This ~55-year timescale is relevant for:
    #   - Nebular dynamics and magnetic field evolution
    #   - Galactic disk magnetic cycle stability
    #   - Quasar jet energy dissipation over cosmic time
    #
    # Role in Universal Magnetism (Um):
    #   Time factor = 1 - e^(-γt × cos(πt_n))
    #
    #   This factor introduces RECIPROCATING behavior:
    #   - When cos(πt_n) > 0: exponent negative → DECAY
    #   - When cos(πt_n) < 0: exponent positive → GROWTH
    #   - cos(πt_n) oscillates with period 2 days in t_n
    #
    # Behavior at key times (with cos(πt_n) = 1):
    #   t = 0:      Factor = 0 (magnetic contribution suppressed)
    #   t = 1000d:  Factor ≈ 0.049 (4.9% of maximum)
    #   t = 20000d: Factor ≈ 0.632 (1-1/e, characteristic time)
    #   t → ∞:      Factor → 1 (full magnetic contribution)
    #
    # Physical Interpretation:
    #   γ governs the temporal decay of magnetic string contributions,
    #   allowing magnetic effects to persist over ~55-year timescales.
    #   The "reciprocation" refers to the cyclic growth/decay behavior
    #   introduced by the cos(πt_n) modulation, which may reflect:
    #   - Time-reversal zones (TRZs)
    #   - Solar/galactic magnetic cycles
    #   - Negentropic processes in biological applications
    #
    # Example (t=1000 days, t_n=0):
    #   γt × cos(πt_n) = 0.00005 × 1000 × 1 = 0.05
    #   e^(-0.05) ≈ 0.951
    #   1 - e^(-0.05) ≈ 0.049
    #   → Only 4.9% of maximum magnetic contribution at t=1000 days
    #
    # ©2025 Daniel T. Murphy, daniel.murphy00@gmail.com – All Rights Reserved
    
